    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Evaluate <m>\oint_C xy~dx + x^2~dy</m> where <m>C</m> is the curve shown and
  oriented in the counterclockwise direction.
            </p>

        </statement>

        
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In a vacuum, Maxwell's equations state that
                <md>
                    <mrow>\nabla \cdot \mbf E \amp = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>\nabla \times \mbf E \amp = -\frac{\partial \mbf B}{\partial t} </mrow>
                    <mrow>\nabla \cdot \mbf B \amp = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>\nabla \times \mbf B \amp = \frac{1}{c^2}\frac{\partial \mbf E}{\partial t} </mrow>
                </md>
                where <m>\mbf E</m> and <m>\mbf B</m> represent the
                time-dependent electric and magnetic field in a region and
                <m>c</m> is the speed of light.
            </p>

            <p>
                Using the results of problem 1, show that
                <md>
                    \nabla^2 \mbf E = \frac{1}{c^2} \frac{\partial^2 \mbf E}{\partial t^2} \ \ \ \text{     and    } \ \ \ \nabla^2 \mbf B = \frac{1}{c^2} \frac{\partial^2 \mbf B}{\partial t^2}.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                (We'll see later that this says <m>\mbf E</m> and <m>\mbf B</m>
                satisfy the <term>wave equation</term>.)
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>\mbf F(x,y,z) = (x,y,z)</m> and <m>\sigma</m> is
                the sphere of radius 2 centered at the origin (
                <m>{x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4}</m>), and <m>\vec n</m> is the outward
                normal vector.
                Evaluate the flux of <m>\mbf F</m> through <m>\sigma</m> in the
                direction of <m>\vec n</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the flux of the field <m>\mbf F=(x,y,0)</m> through the
                surface <m>{z=4-x^2-y^2}</m> that lies above the <m>xy-</m> plane
                with outward normal <m>\vec n</m> that points upward.
                This surface is not closed!
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>